      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2830569872,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "This would be wrong in 50% of the cases making me suspect it would not be super useful.\nWhy not flag both of them as false as a \"don't know which is client\" instead and only have\nthe \"is_client\" features available when we know for sure if it is a client or not?\n\nIf the SYN packets are not seen/available, you can often learn what is client and what is server from higher layer protocols.\nFor example both NFS and SMB could easily indicate back to the tcp layer when they dissect a request or a response packets.\nThis \"is client/is server\" information could be fed back to tcp and other protocols from many many other protocols as well.\n\nMaybe this should not be tracket by TCP at all but rather by \"conversation\". And thus it could be used by TCP for this  but it could also be used for other protocols that might also want to be able to handle client and server traffic differently.\nI.e.  maybe this is useful in a much broader scope than just for tcp connections.",
          "author": {
            "id": 7014716,
            "username": "sahlberg",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Ronnie Sahlberg",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/88911e6cfe3dfc07b327385209d35a46898b5f12538fcb9fe0d5cf97dd7b94d2?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/sahlberg"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-10-19T23:24:40.892Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-10-19T23:31:01.542Z",
